16.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The board is not free to attack. The white stones D1–D2 are in atari: D1 is next to C1 black and D2 white, D2 is next to C2 black, D1 white and D3 empty. The only liberty of the D1–D2 group is D3, so unless White plays D3 on the first move Black can capture D1–D2 with B D3. The survival necessity removes the whole left-side net as a first-move candidate.

On the left side Black has
G1={A2} L={A1,A3,B2}
G2={B3,B4} L={A3,A4,B2}
G3={C1,C2} L={B1,B2}
with B2 the unique common liberty of the three groups.

The suicide argument used for W B1 is false under proper group liberties. After W B1 the reply B B2 is legal: B2 joins A2, B3 and C2, the merged group M={A2,B3,B4,C1,C2,B2} has liberties A1,A3,A4, so B2 is not suicide. M then puts the white B1 stone in atari on A1. The same group-liberty effect shows that W B2 is a mutual atari: W B2 puts W B2 and C1–C2 in atari on B1, and B B1 captures W B2 and leaves C1–C2–B1 with liberties A1,B2. The double atari on B1 is a normal exchange, not a forced kill, and the same mutual-atari logic applies to A3,A4,A1. After any legal first reduction Black can answer with B2 to connect G1,G2,G3 into a three-liberty mass or capture the white stone that created the atari.

Because White is forced to play D3 to avoid losing D1–D2, the first move is essentially forced to D3, and D3 does not create a forced net on any Black group against best defence; Black can keep the merged left side alive with B2 and keep at least two liberties, e.g. A1,A3,A4. Hence no first White move gives a kill that is unavoidable for Black. The claims that W B1 is a forced kill rely on the mistaken premise that B B2 is a suicide; it is not. The correct set of forced-kill initiations is empty.

